 :criterion
 {:statement "A FUNDAMENTAL is a definition the theory requires whose CONSTRUCTING SIGNATURE is uninhabited in BOTH Lean and the runtime. Proxies and fixtures do not count as inhabitants."
  :conjunction "BOTH. An object inhabited on one side and not the other is NOT a fundamental under this criterion; it is a wiring hole, and four such are recorded below with verdict :out so the boundary is legible rather than implied."
  :what-counts-as-an-inhabitant
  ["Lean: a non-private declaration whose value is built from the machine's own declared model inputs (a GenerativeModel, a BeliefState, a declared policy carrier), not from types introduced for the witness."
   "Runtime: a value the shipped code computes from machine data, reachable on a code path (a default-off flag still counts as reachable; a namespace with no caller does not)."]
  :what-does-not-count
  [":fixture -- a hand-built value in a *Witness.lean or *Negative.lean module, whose Policy/Obs/State/Parameter types are declared in that module for the witness. Every carrier in the spine has one; if fixtures counted, the census would be empty and would say nothing."
   ":private-counterexample -- a private Lean value built to state a refutation (eigCounterPredictive)."
   ":proxy -- a runtime value of a DIFFERENT grain or a different functional form that stands in for the required object, and that the code itself usually says is a stand-in."]
  :why-fixtures-are-excluded "The exclusion is the ruling's, and it has teeth here: PredictiveOutcomeKernelWitness.predictive has TWO policy rows and proves all rows normalise, so a criterion that counted fixtures would report Q(o|pi) as inhabited and close the finding the ruling was issued about. What is missing is not a kernel value; it is a MAP from the machine's model and belief to one."
  :limits-of-the-criterion "Uninhabitedness is DECLARED here, not decided. Lean cannot decide it (see :derivation-proposal :what-lean-cannot-do), so each entry's Lean verdict is the result of reading every declaration of the carrier type in the corpus and classifying it, and its falsifier is a single declaration that would refute the reading."}

